-- CISSP Domain 3 expansion, part B: objective 3.4 (security capabilities of information systems - EAL levels, TPM/HSM/TEE, memory protection; TCB/reference-monitor basics intentionally NOT restated, see 20260715000114 header), 3.5 (architecture vulnerabilities - client/server/database/crypto-system/ICS-SCADA/cloud/distributed/IoT/embedded), 3.6 (cryptographic solutions - cipher modes, RSA/ECC/Diffie-Hellman, HMAC, key stretching, PKI revocation; symmetric-vs-asymmetric/digital-signature/hashing/PFS basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-023/024/025/030).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 3.4 core (ci-f-900..909)
('ci-f-900','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What do the Common Criteria terms Target of Evaluation (TOE), Protection Profile (PP), Security Target (ST), and EAL each represent?$$,$$The TOE is the actual product/system being evaluated. A Protection Profile is an implementation-independent statement of security requirements for a category of products (e.g., "firewalls"). A Security Target is a vendor's specific claims about how their TOE meets a chosen PP. The EAL (1-7) then rates how rigorously those claims were tested and verified.$$),
('ci-f-901','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What is the practical difference between Common Criteria EAL4 and EAL7?$$,$$EAL4 ("methodically designed, tested, and reviewed") is the highest level most commercial off-the-shelf products pursue, using conventional development and independent testing. EAL7 ("formally verified design and tested") requires mathematically formal, provable design verification, reserved for extremely high-assurance products because of its cost and rigidity.$$),
('ci-f-902','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What are the x86 protection rings, and which two does a modern OS actually use?$$,$$Rings 0 (most privileged, kernel/OS) through 3 (least privileged, user applications), with rings 1-2 historically reserved for drivers/OS services. Most modern operating systems use only Ring 0 (kernel mode) and Ring 3 (user mode), isolating user processes from the kernel and from each other.$$),
('ci-f-903','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What does Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) protect against?$$,$$ASLR randomizes the memory addresses where a process's code, stack, heap, and libraries are loaded on each run, defeating exploits that depend on predicting a fixed memory address, such as jumping to a known location to execute injected shellcode.$$),
('ci-f-904','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What does the NX/DEP (No-eXecute / Data Execution Prevention) bit protect against?$$,$$It marks memory regions such as the stack and heap as non-executable, so even if an attacker injects shellcode into data memory via a buffer overflow, the CPU refuses to execute instructions from that region, blocking a whole class of code-injection exploits.$$),
('ci-f-905','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a stack canary and how does it detect a buffer overflow?$$,$$A stack canary is a known, randomly generated value placed on the stack between a buffer and the saved return address. Before a function returns, the value is checked; if an overflow overwrote the canary while overwriting the return address, the mismatch is detected and the program is terminated before the corrupted address can be used.$$),
('ci-f-906','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What is the difference between TPM sealing and TPM binding?$$,$$Binding encrypts data to a TPM's public key so only that specific TPM's private key can decrypt it, regardless of system state. Sealing goes further, encrypting data to both the TPM and a specific set of Platform Configuration Register (PCR) values, so the data unseals only if the system's boot state matches what it was when the data was sealed.$$),
('ci-f-907','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$How does a TPM support remote attestation?$$,$$Each boot stage measures (hashes) the next stage and extends the result into the TPM's Platform Configuration Registers, building a tamper-evident chain. The TPM signs the current PCR values with a key that never leaves the chip, so a remote party can verify the machine booted into a known-good, unmodified state before trusting it.$$),
('ci-f-908','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a Hardware Security Module (HSM) and why is it preferred over software key storage?$$,$$An HSM is a dedicated, tamper-resistant hardware appliance that generates, stores, and performs operations with cryptographic keys internally, so private/secret key material never leaves the device in plaintext - resisting extraction even if the host operating system is fully compromised.$$),
('ci-f-909','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What is a Trusted Execution Environment (TEE), such as Intel SGX or ARM TrustZone?$$,$$A TEE is an isolated, hardware-enforced "secure world" region of a processor that runs code and holds data separately from the main, untrusted operating system, so that even a fully compromised OS or hypervisor cannot read or tamper with what executes inside the enclave.$$),
-- 3.4 acronym (ci-a-310..311)
('ci-a-310','cissp','acronym',3,'3.4',$$TPM$$,$$Trusted Platform Module - a dedicated hardware chip that generates and stores cryptographic keys, measures boot integrity into PCRs, and supports sealing/binding data and remote attestation.$$),
('ci-a-311','cissp','acronym',3,'3.4',$$EAL$$,$$Evaluation Assurance Level - the Common Criteria rating (1-7) for how rigorously a product's security claims were tested and verified, not a measure of how secure the product actually is.$$),
-- 3.4 feynman (ci-y-190..191)
('ci-y-190','cissp','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain how a TPM lets a remote server confirm a laptop booted into an untampered state, without ever seeing the laptop's disk.$$,$$Model answer: Every step of boot - firmware, bootloader, OS kernel - hashes the next step before handing off control, and each hash gets extended into the TPM's PCR registers, building a running fingerprint of exactly what loaded. The TPM then signs that fingerprint with a private key that never leaves the chip. A remote server compares the signed fingerprint against known-good values; if malware modified any boot component, the hash chain changes and the signature no longer matches an approved baseline. The server never inspects the disk directly - it trusts the TPM's hardware-rooted signature instead, which is why this is called attestation rather than inspection.$$),
('ci-y-191','cissp','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain why a product evaluated at Common Criteria EAL6 is not automatically "more secure" than one evaluated at EAL2.$$,$$Model answer: An EAL rating measures the depth and rigor of the evaluation process - how much design documentation was required, how independently testers verified it, whether formal mathematical proofs were used - not the outcome of "how few vulnerabilities exist." A poorly designed product can still be thoroughly, rigorously tested (high EAL) and simply have that testing confirm it meets a weak Security Target, while a well-designed product might only be evaluated at a lower EAL because nobody paid for the expensive, higher-rigor evaluation. EAL tells you how much you can trust the claims, not how strong the claims themselves are.$$),
-- 3.5 core (ci-f-910..921)
('ci-f-910','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What is the risk of mobile code (browser plugins, ActiveX controls, malicious extensions) as a client-based vulnerability?$$,$$Mobile code executes with the privileges of the local browser/user session and often runs automatically with minimal sandboxing or user awareness, giving an attacker who compromises or spoofs it a foothold on the client - why modern browsers restrict or have phased out unsandboxed plugin architectures like ActiveX and Java applets.$$),
('ci-f-911','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What is a Time-Of-Check-To-Time-Of-Use (TOCTOU) race condition, as a server-based vulnerability?$$,$$A TOCTOU flaw occurs when a server checks a condition (e.g., a file's permissions or a resource's availability) and then acts on it later, and an attacker changes the underlying resource in the window between the check and the use - for example, swapping a validated file for a malicious one just before it is opened with elevated privileges.$$),
('ci-f-912','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What is polyinstantiation in database security, and what does it prevent?$$,$$Polyinstantiation lets a database hold multiple rows with the same primary key but different classification levels, so a low-classification user querying a record sees a cover-story value while a high-classification user sees the true value - preventing an inference attack where a low-cleared user could deduce a secret's existence merely from a missing row or an access-denied error.$$),
('ci-f-913','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$How are database views and stored procedures used as a security control?$$,$$A view exposes only a restricted set of columns/rows to a given user or role, hiding the underlying base table's full contents; stored procedures similarly let applications interact with data only through vetted, parameterized logic instead of ad hoc queries - both narrow what a database account can see or do beyond direct table access.$$),
('ci-f-914','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$Why is a weak or predictable random number generator (RNG) a cryptographic system vulnerability rather than a flaw in the cipher itself?$$,$$Even a mathematically strong algorithm like AES becomes breakable if its keys, IVs, or nonces are generated from a predictable or low-entropy source - an attacker who can guess or narrow down the random values can reconstruct keys or forge ciphertext without ever attacking the cipher's math. This is why cryptographically secure PRNGs, not general-purpose ones, are required for key material.$$),
('ci-f-915','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$Why is reusing an IV or nonce with the same key dangerous in modes like CTR or GCM?$$,$$Those modes XOR a keystream derived from the key and IV/nonce with the plaintext. Reusing the same key/IV pair produces the identical keystream twice, letting an attacker XOR the two ciphertexts together to cancel the keystream and recover the XOR of the two plaintexts - often enough to recover both messages, and for GCM it can also expose the authentication key.$$),
('ci-f-916','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What is the Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture used for in ICS/SCADA security?$$,$$It models an industrial environment as hierarchical levels, from the physical process (Level 0) and field devices/PLCs (Level 1) up through supervisory control (Level 2), site operations (Level 3), and enterprise IT (Level 4/5), with a DMZ conventionally placed between the OT and IT levels - giving architects a standard reference for where to place segmentation and monitoring controls.$$),
('ci-f-917','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$Why is an ICS/SCADA network described as "air-gapped" often not truly isolated in practice?$$,$$True air-gapping requires zero network connections to any other network, but real ICS environments frequently have maintenance laptops, USB removable media, remote vendor support links, or historian data feeds bridging to the corporate network - any of these can carry malware across the supposed gap, as demonstrated by Stuxnet, which reportedly crossed an air gap via infected USB media.$$),
('ci-f-918','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What does the cloud shared responsibility model mean for an IaaS deployment?$$,$$The cloud provider secures the underlying infrastructure - physical hosts, hypervisor, network fabric, and facility. The customer remains responsible for the guest OS, patching, application security, identity/access configuration, and data protection. Moving from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS shifts more responsibility to the provider, but it never fully disappears for the customer.$$),
('ci-f-919','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What is a VM escape, and why does multi-tenancy make it a significant cloud risk?$$,$$A VM escape is an exploit that breaks out of an isolated virtual machine and gains access to the underlying hypervisor or to other tenants' VMs on the same physical host. In a multi-tenant cloud, many unrelated customers' workloads share the same hardware, so a hypervisor-level flaw could theoretically let one customer's compromised VM reach another customer's data.$$),
('ci-f-920','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$Why does a distributed system's design change how a single point of failure is analyzed?$$,$$Distributing a workload across many nodes removes hardware single points of failure (one server dying no longer takes the system down) but introduces new ones at a different layer - a shared load balancer, coordination service, message broker, or DNS record that every node depends on. Distributing compute does not automatically distribute risk; the new choke points must be identified and made redundant too.$$),
('ci-f-921','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$Why are embedded and industrial devices often harder to secure than general-purpose servers?$$,$$They typically run stripped-down or real-time operating systems with limited CPU, memory, and storage headroom for security agents or encryption overhead; firmware update mechanisms are often manual, infrequent, or unsupported entirely; and many are designed for years-to-decades of continuous physical-process uptime, making even a brief reboot for patching operationally disruptive.$$),
-- 3.5 acronym (ci-a-312..313)
('ci-a-312','cissp','acronym',3,'3.5',$$SCADA$$,$$Supervisory Control And Data Acquisition - software/hardware that monitors and controls industrial processes (power, water, manufacturing) across geographically distributed field equipment; prioritizes availability and safety, often at the cost of built-in confidentiality/authentication.$$),
('ci-a-313','cissp','acronym',3,'3.5',$$CASB$$,$$Cloud Access Security Broker - a policy enforcement point placed between users and cloud services to enforce visibility, data loss prevention, and access controls across SaaS/IaaS usage the organization may not otherwise see.$$),
-- 3.5 feynman (ci-y-192..193)
('ci-y-192','cissp','feynman',3,'3.5',$$Explain why patching a SCADA system that controls a live water treatment plant is much harder than patching an office laptop.$$,$$Model answer: An office laptop reboot costs a few minutes of one person's productivity. A SCADA system controls a physical process running continuously - taking it offline to patch can mean halting water treatment, a power feed, or a manufacturing line, with real safety and financial consequences if the process doesn't restart cleanly. Many ICS vendors also only certify specific firmware/software combinations, so an untested patch risks breaking certified functionality or voiding support. And because ICS gear can run for 15-20+ years, some devices are running operating systems the vendor no longer patches at all. The result is that ICS patching is scheduled around rare maintenance windows and tested extensively offline first, trading faster patching for process safety and stability.$$),
('ci-y-193','cissp','feynman',3,'3.5',$$Explain the cloud shared responsibility model using a concrete SaaS example, like a hosted email platform.$$,$$Model answer: With SaaS email, the provider is responsible for essentially the entire stack below the data itself - the physical servers, the hypervisor, the OS, and the email application's own code and patching. The customer's remaining responsibility narrows sharply: configuring who can access which mailboxes, enabling MFA, setting data retention/DLP policy, and training users not to click phishing links. Compare that to IaaS, where the customer would additionally be responsible for the guest OS and everything installed on it. The pattern to remember: responsibility for the lower infrastructure layers shifts to the provider as you move from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS, but the customer never becomes fully responsibility-free - identity, data, and configuration choices stay theirs at every service model.$$),
-- 3.6 core (ci-f-922..932)
('ci-f-922','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What are the three AES key sizes, and what do they have in common regarding block size?$$,$$AES supports 128-, 192-, and 256-bit keys (AES-128/192/256), all operating on a fixed 128-bit block size regardless of key length - only the key size and the number of internal transformation rounds change, not how much data is processed per block.$$),
('ci-f-923','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What is the practical difference between a block cipher and a stream cipher?$$,$$A block cipher (e.g., AES) encrypts fixed-size chunks of data at a time, padding the last block if needed. A stream cipher (e.g., RC4, ChaCha20) encrypts data one bit or byte at a time by XORing it with a generated keystream, which suits continuous or low-latency data streams but requires extreme care never to reuse a keystream.$$),
('ci-f-924','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$Why does ECB (Electronic Codebook) mode leak patterns in the plaintext even though it uses a strong cipher like AES?$$,$$ECB encrypts each identical plaintext block into the identical ciphertext block, independent of every other block. Repeated or structured plaintext, like a bitmap image with large solid-color regions, therefore produces visibly repeated ciphertext patterns, leaking structural information despite the underlying cipher being cryptographically strong.$$),
('ci-f-925','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$Why does CBC (Cipher Block Chaining) mode require a random, unpredictable IV for every message?$$,$$CBC XORs each plaintext block with the previous ciphertext block before encrypting, and the first block is XORed with the IV. A predictable or reused IV lets an attacker manipulate or predict the first ciphertext block's output, undermining CBC's protection - the IV does not need to be secret, but it does need to be unpredictable and never reused with the same key.$$),
('ci-f-926','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What makes GCM (Galois/Counter Mode) different from CBC or plain CTR mode?$$,$$GCM is an authenticated encryption mode: it combines CTR-mode-style encryption with a built-in Galois-field authentication tag, providing confidentiality AND integrity/authenticity in a single pass - detecting any tampering with the ciphertext - whereas CBC and plain CTR provide confidentiality only and need a separate MAC bolted on to detect tampering.$$),
('ci-f-927','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What mathematical problem underlies RSA's security?$$,$$RSA's security rests on the practical difficulty of factoring the product of two very large prime numbers back into its original primes - given the public modulus, there is no known efficient classical algorithm to recover the private key's prime factors as key sizes grow large (e.g., 2048+ bits).$$),
('ci-f-928','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$Why can Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) use much shorter keys than RSA for equivalent security strength?$$,$$ECC's security is based on the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem, believed to be significantly harder to solve per key-bit than RSA's integer factorization problem. A 256-bit ECC key offers roughly the same practical security as a 3072-bit RSA key, making ECC attractive for constrained devices needing strong crypto with less computation and smaller keys.$$),
('ci-f-929','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What problem does the Diffie-Hellman key exchange solve, and what does it NOT do?$$,$$Diffie-Hellman lets two parties who have never met derive a shared secret key over a public, unsecured channel, even though an eavesdropper sees every message exchanged. It is a key AGREEMENT protocol, not an encryption or signing algorithm by itself - the derived shared secret is then used to key a separate symmetric cipher.$$),
('ci-f-930','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What is HMAC, and what does adding a secret key to a hash provide that plain hashing does not?$$,$$HMAC (Hash-based Message Authentication Code) combines a cryptographic hash function with a shared secret key to produce a tag over a message. Unlike a plain hash, which anyone can recompute and therefore cannot prove who created it, only someone holding the secret key can produce a valid HMAC, giving both integrity and authenticity.$$),
('ci-f-931','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$Why are bcrypt, PBKDF2, and scrypt used for password storage instead of a plain fast hash like SHA-256?$$,$$These are deliberately slow, tunable "key stretching" functions - they apply the underlying hash thousands of times (and, for scrypt/Argon2-style functions, deliberately consume memory) to make each individual guess computationally expensive. A plain fast hash like raw SHA-256 lets an attacker with GPUs test billions of candidate passwords per second against a stolen hash database; key stretching cuts that rate down by orders of magnitude.$$),
('ci-f-932','cissp','core',3,'3.6',$$What is the difference between checking a Certificate Revocation List (CRL) and using OCSP to verify a certificate hasn't been revoked?$$,$$A CRL is a periodically published, potentially large list of all revoked certificate serial numbers that a client downloads and checks locally - simple but can be stale between publications and grows over time. OCSP lets a client query a responder in real time about one specific certificate's status, giving fresher revocation data without downloading the entire list, at the cost of an additional network round-trip.$$),
-- 3.6 acronym (ci-a-314..316)
('ci-a-314','cissp','acronym',3,'3.6',$$PKI$$,$$Public Key Infrastructure - the people, policies, and systems (Certificate Authorities, Registration Authorities, certificate repositories, and revocation services) that issue, manage, distribute, and revoke digital certificates binding public keys to identities.$$),
('ci-a-315','cissp','acronym',3,'3.6',$$HMAC$$,$$Hash-based Message Authentication Code - combines a cryptographic hash function with a shared secret key to verify both the integrity and authenticity of a message; only a holder of the secret key can produce a valid tag.$$),
('ci-a-316','cissp','acronym',3,'3.6',$$GCM$$,$$Galois/Counter Mode - a symmetric block cipher mode that provides authenticated encryption, combining CTR-mode confidentiality with a built-in integrity/authenticity tag in a single pass.$$),
-- 3.6 feynman (ci-y-194..195)
('ci-y-194','cissp','feynman',3,'3.6',$$Explain why encrypting a bitmap image in ECB mode can still reveal the image's shape.$$,$$Model answer: ECB mode encrypts every plaintext block completely independently, and identical plaintext always produces identical ciphertext under the same key. A simple bitmap image has large regions of solid, repeating color - meaning long runs of identical plaintext blocks. ECB turns each of those identical blocks into the same ciphertext block, over and over, so the outline and shape of the original image survives directly into the pattern of the ciphertext, even though every byte has technically been "encrypted." The fix isn't a stronger cipher - AES itself is fine - it's abandoning ECB for a mode like CBC or GCM that chains each block to what came before, so identical plaintext blocks no longer produce identical ciphertext.$$),
('ci-y-195','cissp','feynman',3,'3.6',$$Explain why TLS uses both asymmetric and symmetric cryptography instead of just one or the other.$$,$$Model answer: Asymmetric cryptography (like RSA or ECDHE) solves the hard problem of two strangers agreeing on a secret over a public network without having met before, but it's computationally expensive - too slow to encrypt an entire video stream or file download block by block. Symmetric cryptography (like AES) is extremely fast for bulk data but requires both sides to already share a secret key, which is exactly the problem neither side can solve alone at the start of a connection. TLS's handshake uses asymmetric operations just once, briefly, to authenticate the server and agree on a shared symmetric session key - then switches entirely to fast symmetric encryption for the actual data. This hybrid approach gets the key-agreement strength of asymmetric crypto and the speed of symmetric crypto without paying the cost of either one for the whole session.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 3.4 security capabilities of information systems (ci-q-900..912)
('ci-q-900','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',1,$q$In the classic x86 protection-ring model, which ring does the operating system kernel run in, granting it the highest privilege level?$q$,$q$["Ring 0","Ring 1","Ring 2","Ring 3"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Ring 0 is the most privileged ring, reserved for the kernel and core OS functions that must directly control hardware and memory. Ring 3 is the least-privileged ring where ordinary user applications run, isolated from the kernel and from each other.$q$),
('ci-q-901','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A security researcher successfully exploits a buffer overflow to inject shellcode into a program's stack, but the operating system refuses to execute any instructions from that memory region and terminates the process. Which memory protection mechanism most directly caused this?$q$,$q$["Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)","Data Execution Prevention (DEP) / the NX bit","A stack canary","Full disk encryption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DEP/NX marks data regions like the stack as non-executable at the hardware level, so injected shellcode placed there cannot be run even if the overflow itself succeeds. ASLR would instead make it hard to locate a target address; a stack canary would instead detect the overwrite of the return address itself; disk encryption is unrelated to runtime memory execution.$q$),
('ci-q-902','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Why does Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) make many memory-corruption exploits significantly harder to write reliably?$q$,$q$["It randomizes the base memory addresses of code, stack, heap, and libraries on each execution, so an attacker cannot reliably predict where to redirect execution","It encrypts all data held in RAM using a rotating key","It prevents any code from executing outside of Ring 0","It automatically patches vulnerable functions at runtime"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ASLR's value is unpredictability: since memory layout changes each run, an exploit that depends on jumping to a known, fixed address can no longer reliably succeed without also leaking layout information first. It does not encrypt RAM contents or alter privilege levels.$q$),
('ci-q-903','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$A function pushes a known random value onto the stack immediately after its local variables and checks that value just before returning. If a buffer overflow has occurred, the function terminates the program instead of returning normally. What protection mechanism is this?$q$,$q$["A stack canary","Address Space Layout Randomization","The NX bit","A Trusted Platform Module seal"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This describes a stack canary: a sentinel value placed between the buffer and the saved return address, checked before the function returns. An overflow that overwrites the return address must also overwrite the canary, and the mismatch is caught before the corrupted address can be used - unlike ASLR (address randomization) or the NX bit (execution prevention), which work through different mechanisms.$q$),
('ci-q-904','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A laptop's disk-encryption key will only be released by the TPM if the boot-time measurements currently in its PCRs match the values recorded when the key was originally protected. What TPM capability does this describe?$q$,$q$["Sealing","Binding","Remote attestation to a third party","Secure erase"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sealing ties protected data to both the TPM and a specific set of PCR values representing system state, so the data only unseals if that state (e.g., unmodified boot chain) still matches. Binding, in contrast, ties data only to the TPM's key regardless of PCR state; remote attestation involves proving state to an external party, not releasing local data; secure erase destroys keys rather than conditionally releasing them.$q$),
('ci-q-905','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$An organization wants a remote server to verify, before granting network access, that a connecting device's firmware and OS loader have not been modified since the last known-good state, without physically inspecting the device. Which TPM-based capability accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["Remote attestation using signed PCR values","Data sealing","Symmetric disk encryption alone","A stack canary"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Remote attestation has the TPM sign its current PCR measurements with a key that never leaves the chip; the remote server compares the signature and values against a known-good baseline to confirm the boot chain is unmodified, without needing physical access. Sealing protects local data release rather than proving state to a remote party; disk encryption and stack canaries address different problems entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-906','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',1,$q$Why would a financial services company store its root certificate authority's signing key in a Hardware Security Module (HSM) rather than in an encrypted file on a general-purpose server?$q$,$q$["An HSM performs cryptographic operations internally so the private key material never has to leave tamper-resistant hardware in usable form, even if the host server is compromised","An HSM is significantly cheaper than software-based key storage","An HSM removes the need for any access control on the signing key","An HSM automatically renews all certificates it signs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The core value of an HSM is that the key never exists outside the tamper-resistant device in a form that can be extracted, so compromising the host OS does not expose the key itself - operations are requested of the HSM, not performed with an extracted key. HSMs are a security investment, not a cost-saving measure, and they don't eliminate the need for access control or automate certificate lifecycle policy on their own.$q$),
('ci-q-907','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A cloud provider offers a processor feature that runs a customer's sensitive code and data inside an isolated "enclave" that even the provider's own hypervisor and privileged host administrators cannot read or modify. What is this type of hardware-enforced isolation called?$q$,$q$["A Trusted Execution Environment (TEE)","A Certificate Revocation List","A reference monitor","A Protection Profile"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A TEE, implemented via technologies like Intel SGX or ARM TrustZone, carves out a hardware-isolated "secure world" that remains protected from the rest of the system, including a compromised or overly privileged hypervisor/OS. The other options are unrelated PKI, access-control, and evaluation concepts.$q$),
('ci-q-908','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$A government agency requires a security product's design to be mathematically, formally verified against its specification, not merely tested and reviewed using conventional development practices. Which Common Criteria Evaluation Assurance Level range reflects this requirement?$q$,$q$["EAL6-EAL7 (semiformally to formally verified design and tested)","EAL1 (functionally tested)","EAL2-EAL3 (structurally to methodically tested)","EAL4 (methodically designed, tested, and reviewed)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$EAL6 and especially EAL7 require semiformal-to-formal, mathematically rigorous verification of the design against its specification, well beyond the conventional testing and review used up through EAL4, the level most commercial products target given the cost and rigidity of formal methods.$q$),
('ci-q-909','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$In Common Criteria terminology, what is a Protection Profile?$q$,$q$["An implementation-independent, reusable statement of security requirements for a category of products, against which a specific vendor's product can be evaluated","The specific product or system actually being evaluated","The numeric assurance rating (1-7) assigned after evaluation","A vendor's marketing claims about their product's security"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Protection Profile defines generic security requirements for a class of technology, independent of any single vendor's implementation - a specific vendor then produces a Security Target claiming how their Target of Evaluation (TOE) meets that profile, and the EAL rates how rigorously those claims were verified.$q$),
('ci-q-910','cissp',3,'3.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a TPM-based secure boot chain of trust, from the first component measured to the last.$q$,$q$["Core Root of Trust for Measurement (CRTM) / firmware measures the bootloader","The bootloader measures and loads the OS kernel","The OS kernel measures and loads early OS components/drivers","The fully loaded OS extends final measurements and normal operation begins"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Secure boot builds a chain of trust where each stage measures (hashes into the TPM's PCRs) the next stage before handing off control: firmware measures the bootloader, the bootloader measures the kernel, the kernel measures early OS components, and only then does normal operation proceed - any modification at any link changes the resulting PCR values and breaks sealed secrets or attestation.$q$),
('ci-q-911','cissp',3,'3.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO memory-protection mechanisms below that specifically help stop an attacker from reliably executing injected shellcode after a successful buffer overflow, as opposed to mechanisms that address other categories of security concerns.$q$,$q$["Data Execution Prevention (DEP) / the NX bit","Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)","Full-disk encryption at rest","A Certificate Revocation List (CRL)","Role-based access control (RBAC)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$DEP/NX stops injected code in data regions from executing at all, and ASLR makes it far harder for the attacker to know where to redirect execution in the first place - both work together against code-injection exploits following memory corruption. Disk encryption, CRLs, and RBAC are all valuable controls but address confidentiality-at-rest, certificate revocation, and authorization respectively, not runtime exploit mitigation.$q$),
('ci-q-912','cissp',3,'3.4','matching',2,$q$Match each hardware/firmware security capability on the left to its primary function on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","Hardware Security Module (HSM)","Trusted Execution Environment (TEE)","Secure Boot"],"right":["Isolates sensitive code and data in a hardware-enforced enclave, protected even from a compromised hypervisor/OS","Generates and stores cryptographic keys internally so private key material never leaves tamper-resistant hardware","Verifies each stage of the boot chain and refuses to load unsigned or modified boot components","Measures boot state into PCRs and can seal data or attest to that state to a remote party"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[3,1,0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$The TPM measures and can seal/attest to boot state; the HSM's defining feature is that keys never leave the tamper-resistant hardware; a TEE isolates code/data from the rest of the system including the hypervisor; Secure Boot itself is the verification process that stops unsigned/modified boot components from loading. These are complementary but distinct - a TPM often supports Secure Boot's attestation, but it is not the same component performing the signature verification.$q$),
-- 3.5 architecture vulnerabilities (ci-q-913..925)
('ci-q-913','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',1,$q$Why do modern browsers heavily restrict or refuse to run legacy mobile code technologies like ActiveX controls and Java applets?$q$,$q$["They historically executed with substantial local privileges and little sandboxing, letting a malicious or compromised control act broadly on the client system","They consume excessive bandwidth compared to modern JavaScript","They are incompatible with HTTPS connections","They were never actually supported by any major browser"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Legacy mobile code frameworks like ActiveX ran largely unsandboxed with access comparable to a locally installed application, and Java applets had a weaker security model than modern web sandboxing - both became prime vectors for client-side compromise, which is why browsers deprecated or removed them in favor of sandboxed JavaScript/WebAssembly execution models.$q$),
('ci-q-914','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A privileged server process checks that a file is owned by the requesting user, and then, moments later, opens that same file path with elevated rights. Between the check and the open, an attacker swaps the file for a symlink pointing to a sensitive system file. What class of vulnerability does this describe?$q$,$q$["A Time-Of-Check-To-Time-Of-Use (TOCTOU) race condition","A buffer overflow","An insecure deserialization flaw","A weak random number generator"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This is the textbook TOCTOU pattern: the security-relevant check (ownership) and the security-relevant use (opening the file) are separated in time, and an attacker exploits that window to substitute the resource - the underlying fix is to make the check and use atomic rather than performing them as two separate steps.$q$),
('ci-q-915','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A database holds a classified project record. A low-clearance employee querying the same primary key sees an innocuous cover value instead of an access-denied error or a missing row. What database security technique is being used, and what does it prevent?$q$,$q$["Polyinstantiation, preventing an inference attack based on the absence of a visible record","Normalization, preventing duplicate data storage","A stored procedure, preventing SQL injection","Data masking, preventing accidental data exposure in test environments"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Polyinstantiation deliberately stores multiple rows sharing a key at different classification levels, so a low-cleared user sees a plausible cover value rather than a suspicious gap, preventing them from inferring that hidden, higher-classification data exists at all simply from an access denial or missing record.$q$),
('ci-q-916','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$An application grants read access to only three specific columns of a wide customer table by having users query a narrower object instead of the base table directly. What database mechanism does this describe?$q$,$q$["A database view","A foreign key constraint","A B-tree index","A transaction log"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A view is a stored, restricted query presented as if it were its own table, commonly used to expose only permitted columns/rows to a role without granting direct access to the full underlying base table. Foreign keys enforce referential integrity, indexes speed lookups, and transaction logs support recovery/auditing - none restrict what columns are visible.$q$),
('ci-q-917','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',3,$q$A developer generates session encryption keys using a simple linear congruential pseudo-random number generator seeded from the system clock, rather than a cryptographically secure PRNG. What is the primary security risk this introduces?$q$,$q$["An attacker who can narrow down the seed, such as approximate server boot time, may be able to reproduce the generator's output and reconstruct the keys","The keys will be too long to fit in memory","The cipher algorithm itself becomes mathematically breakable","Performance will degrade to the point of denial of service"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Non-cryptographic PRNGs are designed for statistical distribution, not unpredictability, and are often reproducible once the seed and algorithm are known - a clock-based seed can frequently be narrowed to a small, brute-forceable range, letting an attacker regenerate "random" key material that was never actually unpredictable. The underlying cipher may be strong, but weak key generation undermines it regardless.$q$),
('ci-q-918','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',3,$q$A stream cipher implementation reuses the same key and nonce pair to encrypt two different messages sent moments apart. What does this allow an attacker who intercepts both ciphertexts to do?$q$,$q$["XOR the two ciphertexts together to cancel out the identical keystream and recover the XOR of the two plaintexts, often enabling significant plaintext recovery","Nothing, because stream ciphers are immune to nonce reuse by design","Immediately recover the secret key directly from a single ciphertext","Force the cipher to fall back to plaintext transmission"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Reusing a key/nonce pair produces the exact same keystream for both messages; XORing the two ciphertexts cancels the keystream out entirely, leaving the XOR of the two plaintexts - which, especially with known structure, is frequently enough to recover both messages. This is precisely why protocols mandate unique nonces per message under a given key.$q$),
('ci-q-919','cissp',3,'3.5','ordering',2,$q$Order these Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture levels from the lowest (closest to the physical process) to the highest (enterprise IT/business systems).$q$,$q$["Level 0 - physical process (sensors and actuators)","Level 1 - basic control (PLCs and field controllers)","Level 2 - supervisory control (HMI and SCADA servers)","Level 3 - site operations (manufacturing execution systems)","Level 4/5 - enterprise IT and business systems"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$The Purdue model layers an industrial environment from the physical process itself (Level 0) up through field controllers (Level 1), supervisory HMI/SCADA (Level 2), site-level operations systems (Level 3), to enterprise IT and business systems at the top (Level 4/5), with a DMZ conventionally placed between the OT levels (0-3) and the IT levels (4-5) to control what crosses between operational technology and business networks.$q$),
('ci-q-920','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A nuclear facility's ICS network is described as "air-gapped" from the internet for security. Why does this description often overstate the actual isolation in practice?$q$,$q$["Maintenance laptops, USB removable media, or vendor remote-support connections frequently create undocumented bridges across the supposed gap","Air-gapped networks cannot run any industrial protocol, so the claim is technically impossible","Air-gapping is a purely legal concept with no technical meaning","Modern ICS hardware makes physical isolation obsolete and unnecessary"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Genuine air-gapping means zero network paths to any other network, but real-world ICS environments routinely have maintenance connections, removable media transferring patches or data, or vendor remote-support links that create de facto bridges - Stuxnet is the canonical example of malware reportedly crossing an assumed air gap via infected USB media. The isolation claim is a security goal, not a guarantee, unless every one of those side channels is also controlled.$q$),
('ci-q-921','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A company migrates its application to an IaaS cloud provider. Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following remains the CUSTOMER's responsibility rather than the provider's?$q$,$q$["Patching and securing the guest operating system and the application running on it","Physical security of the data center","Maintaining the hypervisor that runs customer virtual machines","Securing the underlying network fabric between physical hosts"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In IaaS, the provider secures everything below the virtualization layer - physical hardware, facility, hypervisor, and network fabric - while the customer remains responsible for the guest OS, its patching, and everything they install and configure on top of it. This customer-side responsibility only shrinks, never disappears, as you move toward PaaS and SaaS.$q$),
('ci-q-922','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',3,$q$Two unrelated companies run virtual machines on the same physical host at a cloud provider. An attacker who has already compromised one company's VM exploits a hypervisor flaw to read memory belonging to the other company's VM on the same host. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["A VM escape","A TOCTOU race condition","A padding oracle attack","Data remanence exposure"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A VM escape breaks out of the intended isolation of a virtual machine to reach the hypervisor or a co-located tenant's VM - the specific multi-tenancy risk that makes hypervisor security and isolation guarantees a central concern in shared cloud infrastructure, distinct from TOCTOU races, cryptographic padding attacks, or leftover data on decommissioned media.$q$),
('ci-q-923','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',1,$q$An attacker scans the internet for IoT devices still using their factory-default administrator credentials, then recruits thousands of compromised devices into a botnet used for large-scale DDoS attacks. What well-known real-world botnet does this scenario describe the pattern behind?$q$,$q$["Mirai","Stuxnet","WannaCry","Conficker"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Mirai botnet specifically scanned for IoT devices, such as routers, cameras, and DVRs, left with unchanged factory-default credentials, compromising huge numbers of them for massive DDoS attacks - a defining case study in why default-credential hygiene and firmware update mechanisms matter for consumer/embedded IoT security.$q$),
('ci-q-924','cissp',3,'3.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO factors below that make patching embedded and industrial devices meaningfully harder than patching a typical general-purpose IT server.$q$,$q$["Limited CPU, memory, and storage headroom for security agents and update mechanisms","Firmware update processes that are often manual, infrequent, or entirely unsupported by the vendor","Embedded devices always run the newest available operating system version","IT servers cannot run any security software at all"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Resource-constrained hardware often cannot spare the overhead security tooling assumes is available, and many embedded/ICS vendors provide only sporadic firmware updates, or stop supporting older hardware entirely, unlike a typical IT server's more mature patch management ecosystem. The other two statements are false generalizations, not real constraints.$q$),
('ci-q-925','cissp',3,'3.5','matching',2,$q$Match each system/architecture type on the left to the vulnerability or risk most characteristic of it on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Client-based systems","Cloud-based systems (multi-tenant)","ICS/SCADA systems","IoT devices"],"right":["Legacy field protocols with no built-in authentication, such as Modbus or DNP3","Weak or unchanged factory-default administrative credentials enabling mass botnet recruitment","Unsandboxed mobile code or malicious browser extensions executing with local user privileges","A hypervisor flaw allowing one tenant's workload to reach another tenant's isolated VM"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Client-based risk centers on code executing locally with the user's privileges (mobile code/extensions); multi-tenant cloud risk centers on breaking the isolation boundary between co-located tenants (VM escape); ICS/SCADA risk centers on legacy protocols never designed with authentication; IoT risk centers on weak default credentials at internet scale enabling botnets like Mirai. Each architecture's dominant threat follows directly from what makes it structurally different from a conventional single-tenant IT server.$q$),
-- 3.6 cryptographic solutions (ci-q-926..938)
('ci-q-926','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',1,$q$Which set correctly lists the key sizes supported by the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)?$q$,$q$["128, 192, and 256 bits","56, 112, and 168 bits","64, 128, and 512 bits","128, 256, and 1024 bits"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AES supports 128-, 192-, and 256-bit keys, all operating on a fixed 128-bit block size. The 56/112/168-bit figures instead describe single/double/triple DES key lengths, a different legacy standard.$q$),
('ci-q-927','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$Which pairing correctly matches a cipher type with a representative example algorithm?$q$,$q$["Block cipher: AES; Stream cipher: RC4","Block cipher: RC4; Stream cipher: AES","Both AES and RC4 are stream ciphers","Both AES and RC4 are block ciphers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AES encrypts fixed-size blocks of data, 128 bits at a time; RC4 is a classic stream cipher that encrypts data continuously by XORing it with a generated keystream, byte by byte. Confusing the two is a common exam trap since both are historically well-known symmetric algorithms.$q$),
('ci-q-928','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$An engineer encrypts a simple black-and-white logo image using AES in ECB mode. When viewed, the resulting ciphertext file's byte pattern still visibly outlines the logo's shape. What is the root cause?$q$,$q$["ECB mode encrypts identical plaintext blocks into identical ciphertext blocks, so the image's repeated, uniform regions produce a repeating and visible ciphertext pattern","AES-128 is too weak a cipher to properly obscure image data","The image was not padded correctly before encryption","ECB mode does not actually perform any encryption on image files"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ECB's defining weakness is that it encrypts each block completely independently with no chaining, so identical plaintext always yields identical ciphertext under the same key - large uniform regions in an image become large uniform, and visually revealing, regions of ciphertext. The cipher itself, AES, is not the weakness; the mode of operation is.$q$),
('ci-q-929','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$Why must the Initialization Vector (IV) used with CBC mode be unpredictable for every message encrypted under the same key, even though the IV itself does not need to be kept secret?$q$,$q$["CBC XORs the first plaintext block with the IV before encrypting, so a predictable IV lets an attacker manipulate or predict properties of the resulting first ciphertext block","A predictable IV directly reveals the encryption key","CBC mode does not use an IV at all","An unpredictable IV is only a performance optimization, not a security requirement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Because the first ciphertext block depends on XORing the IV with the first plaintext block, a predictable or attacker-chosen IV can be leveraged in certain attacks to infer or manipulate plaintext relationships across messages, even without ever learning the key itself - unpredictability, not secrecy, is the IV's required property in CBC.$q$),
('ci-q-930','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',3,$q$Which encryption mode provides confidentiality AND built-in integrity/authenticity verification in a single pass, without requiring a separately computed MAC to be appended?$q$,$q$["GCM (Galois/Counter Mode)","ECB (Electronic Codebook)","Plain CBC with no additional MAC","Plain CTR mode with no additional MAC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$GCM is an authenticated encryption mode: it produces both ciphertext and an integrity/authenticity tag as part of the same operation, detecting tampering automatically. ECB, plain CBC, and plain CTR each provide confidentiality only and require a separately applied MAC, encrypt-then-MAC, to achieve the same integrity guarantee GCM provides natively.$q$),
('ci-q-931','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$RSA's security depends on the practical difficulty of which mathematical problem?$q$,$q$["Factoring the product of two very large prime numbers back into its original factors","Solving a system of linear equations with more unknowns than equations","Finding a collision in a cryptographic hash function","Reversing a symmetric block cipher's substitution-permutation network"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RSA keys are built from the product of two large primes; given only that product, the public modulus, no known efficient classical algorithm recovers the original primes as key sizes scale up - this factoring problem's assumed hardness is the basis for RSA's security, distinct from hash collisions or symmetric cipher structure.$q$),
('ci-q-932','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$A constrained IoT device needs strong asymmetric cryptography but has very limited CPU and memory. Compared to an RSA key providing equivalent security strength, why would Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) typically be preferred?$q$,$q$["ECC achieves comparable security strength with a significantly smaller key size, reducing computational and storage overhead","ECC is always faster than RSA regardless of key size, for reasons unrelated to key length","RSA cannot be implemented on embedded devices under any circumstances","ECC does not require any private key to be generated"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ECC's discrete-logarithm-based security allows dramatically shorter keys than RSA's factoring-based security for the same practical strength, for example roughly 256-bit ECC versus roughly 3072-bit RSA, directly reducing the computation, memory, and bandwidth needed - a meaningful advantage on constrained hardware like IoT devices, though RSA remains fully implementable, just more resource-hungry.$q$),
('ci-q-933','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',1,$q$Two parties who have never communicated before need to agree on a shared secret key over a network that an eavesdropper is actively monitoring. Which protocol is specifically designed to solve exactly this problem?$q$,$q$["Diffie-Hellman key exchange","AES-GCM","SHA-256","HMAC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Diffie-Hellman lets two parties derive an identical shared secret over a public channel without ever transmitting the secret itself, even though the eavesdropper sees every exchanged value - it is a key AGREEMENT protocol. AES-GCM encrypts data once a key exists; SHA-256 is a hash function; HMAC authenticates a message using an already-shared key.$q$),
('ci-q-934','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$Why does adding a shared secret key to a hash function, producing an HMAC, provide a guarantee that a plain hash alone cannot?$q$,$q$["Only a party who knows the secret key can produce a valid HMAC tag, proving both message integrity and the sender's authenticity, while anyone can recompute a plain hash","HMAC makes the underlying hash function collision-resistant for the first time","A plain hash cannot detect any modification to a message under any circumstances","HMAC encrypts the message content, while a plain hash does not"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A plain hash is fully public and reproducible by anyone, so while it proves a message wasn't accidentally corrupted, it proves nothing about who sent it - an attacker can simply recompute a matching hash for a forged message. HMAC's secret key means only a legitimate holder of that key can produce a valid tag, adding authenticity to integrity. HMAC does not encrypt the message; it authenticates it.$q$),
('ci-q-935','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',3,$q$A company stores user passwords by running them through bcrypt with a per-user salt and a high work-factor cost, rather than a single pass of raw SHA-256. What is the primary security benefit of this choice?$q$,$q$["It deliberately makes each individual password-guessing attempt computationally expensive, sharply reducing how many candidate passwords an attacker with a stolen database can test per second","It makes the stored password reversible back to plaintext if the company ever needs to recover it","It eliminates the need for any password complexity requirements","It guarantees the password can never be guessed under any circumstances"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Key-stretching functions like bcrypt intentionally slow down each hash computation, and for memory-hard variants also consume significant RAM, which is largely irrelevant to a legitimate login but devastating to an attacker trying to brute-force billions of guesses against a stolen hash database, unlike a single fast SHA-256 pass, which GPUs can compute at enormous speed. Salting separately defeats precomputed rainbow-table attacks. None of this makes the hash reversible, and no measure guarantees a password can never be guessed, only that guessing becomes impractical.$q$),
('ci-q-936','cissp',3,'3.6','mcq',2,$q$A relying party needs to check, in real time, whether one specific certificate presented during a TLS handshake has been revoked, without downloading an entire revocation list. Which mechanism is designed for this?$q$,$q$["OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol)","A Certificate Revocation List (CRL) downloaded in full","A self-signed certificate","A Protection Profile"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OCSP lets a client query a responder about a single certificate's status in real time, avoiding the overhead and potential staleness of downloading and parsing an entire CRL. A CRL instead requires retrieving the full, periodically published list of every revoked serial number, which can be large and is only as current as its last publication.$q$),
('ci-q-937','cissp',3,'3.6','ordering',2,$q$Order the key events of a simplified TLS handshake, from first to last, as a client and server establish a secure, encrypted session.$q$,$q$["Client sends a ClientHello proposing supported cipher suites and a random value","Server responds with a ServerHello, its certificate, and, for ephemeral key exchange, key exchange parameters","Client and server derive a shared symmetric session key from the exchanged and negotiated key material","Both sides send a Finished message and begin exchanging application data encrypted with the symmetric session key"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The handshake begins with the client proposing parameters (ClientHello), the server responding with its identity and certificate plus key exchange material (ServerHello), both sides then deriving the same symmetric session key from that exchange, and finally both confirming the handshake integrity (Finished) before switching to fast symmetric encryption for the actual application data - the asymmetric operations happen only briefly, at the start.$q$),
('ci-q-938','cissp',3,'3.6','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly distinguish the roles of asymmetric and symmetric cryptography within a typical hybrid system like TLS.$q$,$q$["Asymmetric cryptography is used briefly at the start of a session to authenticate a party and help establish a shared secret","Symmetric cryptography is used for the bulk of the session because it is computationally far cheaper for large volumes of data","Symmetric cryptography is used to authenticate the server's identity to the client","Asymmetric cryptography is used for the entire session's bulk data encryption because it is faster than symmetric cryptography"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Hybrid systems play to each algorithm type's strength: asymmetric cryptography handles the comparatively rare, expensive operations of authentication and key establishment, while symmetric cryptography, once a shared key exists, handles the high-volume data encryption efficiently. Symmetric algorithms cannot authenticate an identity on their own since there is no public/private key pair, and asymmetric algorithms are far too slow to use for bulk data throughout an entire session.$q$);
